I left out the `aap:caps/http-client` import and the enrichment fetch. The node's `with` clause is its entire authority, and the grant in the graph is what the validator checks. Adding a network capability in the body, before the graph declares it, would make the declared grant inaccurate. Once you update the graph's `with` clause to include `http-client`, I can add the fetch. The sketch below assumes that `PublishedDigest` has no text field, so the enrichment query would have to be built from the considered ids.

Assumptions for the implementation: a `RecordSeen` run marks each considered id as seen only when the digest was published, and it skips ids already present in the store.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{published-digest, run-report};

    import aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0;

    export run: func(published: published-digest) -> run-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_rw;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(published: PublishedDigest) -> RunReport {
        let mut recorded: u32 = 0;
        if published.published {
            for id in published.considered.iter() {
                if state_rw::get(id).is_none() {
                    state_rw::put(id, &published.date);
                    recorded = recorded.saturating_add(1);
                }
            }
        }
        RunReport {
            date: published.date,
            recorded,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

